        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Holders</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these holders. They were chosen from published specifications, and
            the claims on the cards are limited to what two sources for each product support: the
            maker's own store and the Amazon listing for the same model number, both checked on 15
            October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the mounting points a treadmill actually offers: round side handrails,
            the front crossbar or grab bar, the console upright and, for walking pads without
            handrails, a desk beside the machine. For each, we looked for a holder that is on sale
            now, that publishes both the device sizes it grips and the bar diameters it clamps, and
            whose figures agree between the maker and a retailer. Where the two described a figure
            differently, we left it out of the specification line.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we dropped, and why</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Two RAM Mounts kits</strong>, which use RAM's rubber ball-and-socket arms.
            RAM's own store lists its X-Grip Handlebar U-Bolt Mount for 9 to 11 inch tablets
            (RAM-B-149Z-UN9U) as in stock, but the Amazon listing for that model showed it as
            currently unavailable, and the retailer descriptions we found give a different tablet
            size from RAM's. We could not see a second current price. RAM's X-Grip with Tough-Claw
            for 7 to 8 inch tablets with the long arm (RAM-B-400-C-UN8U) did not appear under that
            model number at Amazon or the other major retailers we tried, and reseller pages for
            similar Tough-Claw kits give a different clamp range and different prices. Both may well
            be good holders; we could not cross-check them.</li>
            <li><strong>Arkon's exercise-equipment version of its clamp mount</strong> (EXBK131),
            which Arkon sells for bikes, treadmills and ellipticals. We could not find it on Amazon
            under that model number, and while a retailer listing agreed with its fit ranges, we
            could not confirm that retailer's price or stock. The TAB131, which Arkon and Amazon both
            describe with the same screen range and clamp range, is listed instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Most marketplace "treadmill tablet holders."</strong> A large share give no
            bar range at all, or give a device range with no maker information we could check
            against. Acrylic ledges that rest a tablet on the console, and floor stands, were also
            left out: the first does not grip the device, and most treadmill layouts have no room for
            the second.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One card needed a judgement call. For the SM634 phone mount, Arkon gives the device range
            as a phone height and the Amazon listing gives it as a screen size, and the two do not
            line up neatly. Both agree on the bar range and the price, so the holder stays, but the
            device range is left off its card.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety actions.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
            recall database for Arkon and RAM Mounts, and for tablet holders, phone holders and
            mounts generally, and its website for safety warnings, and found no recall or warning
            covering any holder on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-mount" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where to Mount It: Handrail, Crossbar or Console</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill offers different bars, and the choice of bar matters more than the
            choice of holder. Work through three questions in order, before you order anything.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. Does it keep the controls clear?</h3>
          <p>
            The safety key, its clip and cord, and the stop button must stay visible and reachable
            with the tablet in place, in both portrait and landscape. The same goes for the speed
            and incline keys you use most, and for any quick-stop or emergency button on the
            handrails. A holder above the console must not let the tablet hang down over the key,
            and one on a side rail must not swing into the cord's path when you grab the rail. If
            you cannot pull the key or press stop without touching the tablet, move the holder.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety</a>
            covers why the key belongs clipped to your clothing every session, and a screen is
            exactly the kind of distraction that makes it matter.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. Is the bar fixed?</h3>
          <p>
            Clamp only to a part of the frame that does not move during a workout or when the
            treadmill folds. Never mount anything on the belt, the deck, the motor hood, the
            incline mechanism, a folding hinge or the lift handle, and never on a bar that pivots,
            slides or swings. A holder on a moving part can work loose, pinch a cable, crack the
            plastic it is clamped to, or drop into the belt, and a falling tablet is the least of
            those problems.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. Is it solid at running pace?</h3>
          <p>
            Grab the bar you have in mind and shake it. A front crossbar tied into both uprights
            usually moves least. A long side rail can flex in the middle, and a console upright can
            pass on vibration from the motor and the deck. Mount as close as you can to where the
            bar is supported, and keep the holder's arm short.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Handrail versus console mounting</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Side handrail.</strong> Easy to clamp, and usually round, which suits a screw
            clamp. The catch is position: the screen ends up to one side, which means turning your
            head, and it sits where your hand naturally goes when you steady yourself. It works for a
            phone used for music or a timer, and for walking; for watching a tablet at running pace
            it is the weakest spot on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Front crossbar or grab bar.</strong> Usually the best spot for a tablet: centred,
            often closer to eye level than the console, and fixed at both ends. Check that the
            tablet in landscape does not cover the console's display or the controls beneath it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On or above the console.</strong> Built-in ledges and media shelves are fine for
            walking if they do not cover the controls, but most hold the tablet low and loose. For
            running, a clamp mount on a bar above the console is steadier than a tablet resting on a
            ledge, and keeps the console readable. Do not clamp to the console's plastic housing, its
            fan grille or its trim; consoles are not built to carry a load on an arm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Screen Height, Distance and Neck Posture</h2>
<p>
            The commonest complaint about watching a tablet on a treadmill is a stiff neck, and the
            usual cause is a screen that is too low. Most consoles put the tablet ledge well below
            eye level, so you spend the session looking down at it.
          </p>
          <p>
            As general set-up guidance, aim for the top of the screen to sit roughly at eye level
            when you stand tall on the belt, so you look straight ahead or very slightly down. That
            also helps you hold your position in the middle of the belt, because looking down tends
            to pull your stride forward towards the motor hood. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill running form</a>
            explains why where you look matters for the rest of your posture.
          </p>
          <p>
            Distance is the other half. Too close, and the screen bounces in your field of view with
            every stride and you find yourself checking your feet against it; too far, and you lean
            towards it to read. Somewhere around arm's length works for most people watching video.
            For reading, a larger font or a zoomed view matters more than a closer screen, and
            subtitles are easier to follow than small print at any distance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three details catch people out:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Incline.</strong> As the deck rises, you rise with it relative to the
            console, so a screen that sits at eye level on the flat ends up below it at a steep
            incline. Set the height at the incline you use most.</li>
            <li><strong>Different users.</strong> A screen set for one person is wrong for someone
            much taller or shorter. A ball joint or an adjustable arm makes changing over quick;
            mark the positions if several people share the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Glare.</strong> A window or a ceiling light behind you reflects off the
            screen and makes you crane to see past it. Tilt the screen slightly, or move the light,
            before you move the holder.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this is medical advice. If you have neck or back pain, or a condition that
            affects balance, ask a qualified professional how you should set up and use a
            treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Vibration and Wobble at Running Pace</h2>
<p>
            Almost any holder is steady at a walk. Running is where they differ, and the cause is
            usually the mount and the bar, not the cradle that grips the tablet.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Arm length.</strong> Every extra inch of arm magnifies movement at the
            screen. Use the shortest arm that gets the screen where you want it. That is why the
            long-arm desk mount on this page is listed for walking pads, not for running.</li>
            <li><strong>Clamp fit.</strong> A clamp in the middle of its range grips well; one
            stretched to its maximum, or packed out with a folded cloth, does not. If your bar is at
            the very edge of a holder's published range, choose a different holder rather than
            improvising.</li>
            <li><strong>Clamp type.</strong> A screw clamp that closes on a round bar generally
            twists less than a strap. Straps fit more bar sizes and shapes, which is their point,
            but they are better suited to walking and easy running than to speed work.</li>
            <li><strong>Ball joints and knobs</strong> loosen with vibration. Check and retighten
            them every week or two, and if the screen droops mid-run, stop the belt and fix it
            rather than reaching forward to push it back.</li>
            <li><strong>The tablet itself.</strong> A heavy tablet in a rugged case puts more load on
            a light composite holder. If yours is heavy, keep the arm short and the clamp close to
            an upright.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Some screen shake comes from the treadmill rather than the holder. A machine that thuds,
            rocks or rattles at speed will pass that into anything clamped to it, and no holder can
            cancel it out. If the whole frame moves, fix that first: our guides to
            <a href="/treadmill-shaking-or-wobbling/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a treadmill that shakes or wobbles</a>
            and
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill thumping noises</a>
            cover the usual causes, from uneven feet to a belt that needs attention.
          </p>
          <p>
            A simple test before you trust a set-up: mount the holder with the tablet in it, start
            the belt at your normal running speed, and stand on the side rails watching the screen.
            If it moves enough to blur text from there, it will be worse with you running on the
            belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mount-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Clamp, Strap or Desk Mount: Reading a Listing</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill tablet holders come in three basic forms, and the right one depends on your
            bar rather than your tablet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Screw clamps</strong> close around a round bar with a knob or a screw. They are
            the steadiest of the three on a bar that sits inside their range, and they are what most
            people with a round crossbar or handrail should buy. Their weakness is shape: a square,
            oval or very thick bar may not fit at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strap mounts</strong> pull a strap tight around the bar. They cover a wider range
            of sizes, fit some bars a clamp will not, and come off quickly, which suits a shared or
            folding machine. They generally allow a little more twist than a clamp.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Desk and C-clamp mounts</strong> grip a flat edge, such as a desk or worktop, and
            usually carry the tablet on a longer arm. They are for walking pads and treadmill desks,
            where there is no bar to clamp to, not for the treadmill frame itself.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The three figures that matter</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Device size.</strong> Listings quote either a screen size range, such as 7 to
            18.4 inches, or a physical range for the device's height or width. Screen size is a
            loose guide, because two tablets with the same screen can differ in overall size, and a
            rugged case adds more. Where a maker publishes physical dimensions, measure your device
            in its case against those. When the maker and a retailer describe the range in different
            ways, as with one of our picks, measure and compare against the maker's figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bar diameter.</strong> The single most useful figure for a treadmill, and the
            one most often missing. Measure round the bar with a tape and divide by about 3.14, or
            use calipers. Note whether the bar is round, oval or square, because a clamp range
            usually assumes round tube.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight limit.</strong> Few holders publish one, and where a retailer lists one
            the maker often does not. A large tablet in a heavy case is at the top of what light
            composite holders are designed for, so keep the arm short and the clamp close to a
            support.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be sceptical of "universal". It usually means a wide range that fits many devices, and
            fits the ones at either end of it less securely. And be sceptical of listings that name
            the treadmill brands a holder suits without giving a bar diameter: a brand name tells you
            nothing about the bar on your model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Folding Treadmills and Walking Pads</h2>
<p>
            On a folding treadmill, check what moves when the deck lifts. The deck rises towards
            the console, and on some machines it ends up close to the front crossbar or the console
            itself. A holder and tablet left in place can be struck by the deck, can stop it folding
            fully and latching, or can be crushed between the two. The safe routine is to fold the
            machine once with the holder fitted and no tablet in it, slowly, watching for contact,
            and then to take the tablet off every time you fold, whatever the clearance looked like.
            A holder with a quick-release grip or a strap you can undo in seconds makes that habit
            easier to keep.
          </p>
          <p>
            Never clamp to the deck frame, the lift handle or the hinge area, all of which move when
            the machine folds, and do not leave a charging cable running from the tablet to a
            socket across the fold line. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-fold-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding a treadmill safely</a>
            covers the latch and the hydraulic assist, which is where fingers and cables get caught.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking pads without handrails have nowhere to clamp at all, and clamping anything to
            the pad's frame puts it at floor level next to the belt. A desk, a wall mount or a shelf
            at eye height is the answer there, which is why our walking-pad pick is a desk mount. If
            you walk at a standing desk, our
            <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk guide</a>
            covers screen positions for working while you walk. Walking pads with a fold-up handle
            bar are a middle case: the bar is usually thin and folds down, so treat it as a moving
            part and use a desk or wall mount instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setting-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Fitting a Holder: A Five-Minute Check</h2>
<p>
            Most problems with treadmill tablet holders show up in the first session. Running this
            check once, with the machine unplugged at the wall for the fitting itself, catches nearly
            all of them.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Measure first.</strong> Bar diameter, bar shape, and your device in its case.
            Compare all three with the holder's published ranges before you order.</li>
            <li><strong>Fit the clamp or strap</strong> to a fixed bar, close to an upright, and
            tighten it until it does not rotate when you push the holder firmly by hand. Use the
            maker's own pads or inserts; do not add your own packing.</li>
            <li><strong>Insert the device</strong> in the orientation you will use, and set the
            support legs or grips so side buttons and ports stay clear.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the controls.</strong> With the device in place, reach for the safety
            key and the stop button. If the screen is in the way of either, or of the cord, move
            the holder.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the cable.</strong> If you charge while you run, route the cable along
            the frame and away from the belt, the motor hood and the fold line. A loop of cable
            hanging towards the deck can be pulled in by the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Plug in and test at speed</strong>, standing on the side rails, at the speed
            and incline you normally use. Watch for shake, droop and glare.</li>
            <li><strong>Fold it</strong>, if the treadmill folds, with the device removed, and check
            the deck clears the holder.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            After that, a quick look at the knob or strap every week or two is enough. Any holder
            that has started to slip on its bar should come off and go back on properly before the
            next session; a slow creep downwards is how a tablet ends up over the controls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Using a Tablet Without Losing Your Footing</h2>
<p>
            A tablet holder makes it easier to watch something and harder to notice your feet.
            Three habits keep it safe.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Set everything up before you start the belt.</strong> Choose the episode,
            the playlist or the class standing on the side rails, and set the volume.</li>
            <li><strong>Slow down to touch the screen.</strong> Reaching forward to tap a tablet at
            running pace is a reliable way to drift towards the motor hood. Drop to a walk first,
            or use headphone controls instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the safety key clipped on.</strong> The more absorbing the screen, the
            more it matters.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Follow-along classes are worth a thought here. A class that asks you to change speed and
            incline often will have you looking down at the console more than a film will, so a
            screen that sits close above the console works better for classes than one mounted
            high. For ideas on what to watch, and how to structure a session so the screen helps
            rather than distracts, see our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-make-treadmill-running-less-boring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making treadmill running less boring</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sound, cables and the end of the session</h3>
          <p>
            Treadmills are noisy, and turning a tablet's speaker up to compete with the motor and
            the belt is one more reason to lean in towards it. Wireless headphones solve that and
            keep a cable out of the way; if you use wired ones, run the lead inside your top rather
            than across the console, where it can catch on the safety key cord or your hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            Charging cables deserve the same care. A tablet that needs charging during a long
            session should be charged from a socket at the front of the machine, with the cable
            running along a fixed part of the frame and nowhere near the belt, the rollers or the
            motor hood. Never run it underneath the treadmill or across the space behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the end of a session, stop the belt fully before you reach for the tablet, and step
            off onto the side rails first. It sounds obvious, but the moment people most often
            reach for a screen is the moment the workout ends, while the belt is still slowing down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your treadmill has a tablet ledge at a good height</strong> that clears the
            controls, and you mostly walk, you may not need a holder at all. Try the ledge for a
            week before buying anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you only listen</strong>, a phone in a pocket, a belt pouch or an armband is
            simpler than any mount, and keeps the console clear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your treadmill already has a large touchscreen</strong>, check what it can
            show before adding a second screen above it. A tablet clamped over a built-in display
            can block the very screen the machine's classes run on, and a holder hung from the
            console's frame may put load where the maker never intended. If you do add one, mount
            it to the side of or below the built-in screen on a fixed bar, never on the display
            housing itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a big screen</strong>, a wall-mounted television in front of the
            treadmill is usually easier on your neck than any tablet, provided it sits at eye level
            rather than above you, and its cable runs nowhere near the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your machine has no fixed bar within the published ranges</strong> — an
            unusually thick, square or sculpted frame — do not force a holder onto it. A desk or wall
            mount beside the treadmill is the safer choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are kitting out a new machine</strong>, our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories guide</a>
            covers the mat, lubricant and other things worth buying before a tablet holder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a tablet holder on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a fixed part of the frame, usually the front crossbar, where the screen can sit near eye level without covering the safety key, the stop button or the speed controls. Avoid long side rails that flex, and never clamp to the deck, motor hood, incline mechanism, lift handle or folding hinge, which move during use or when the treadmill folds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my iPad shaking on the treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the shortest arm that puts the screen where you want it, clamp close to where the bar is supported, and make sure the clamp suits the bar's diameter rather than being stretched to its limit. Screw clamps generally twist less than straps. Retighten knobs and ball joints regularly, and check whether the treadmill itself rocks or rattles at speed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my phone on the treadmill safely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is held in a mount rather than in your hand, and you set up what you want to watch or hear before starting the belt. Slow to a walk before touching the screen, keep the safety key clipped on, and make sure the mount does not cover the stop button or the key, or sit where your hand lands on the rail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What height should a treadmill tablet be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As general set-up guidance, roughly at eye level when you stand tall on the belt, so you look straight ahead or slightly down rather than at the console. Check it at the incline you use most, because the deck lifts you. If you have neck or back problems, ask a qualified professional about the right set-up for you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a tablet holder fit my treadmill handrail?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure the rail's diameter and shape and compare them with the bar range the holder's maker publishes. The clamp mounts on this page take round bars from 12 to 40 mm, and the strap mounts from 15 to 50 mm. Square, oval or sculpted rails may need a strap, or a desk mount instead. If a listing gives no bar range, choose another holder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a tablet holder if my treadmill has a media shelf?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. If the shelf holds the tablet at a comfortable height, keeps it clear of the controls and the tablet stays put at your usual pace, it is fine. Many shelves are low or let the tablet slide when you run, which is when a clamp-on holder on a bar above the console is worth buying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Tablet Holders</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come down to mounting position, not to the holder itself.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering the safety key or stop button</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A tablet mounted above the console can hang down over the key and the stop button, especially in landscape. Check with the tablet in place that you can pull the key and press stop without touching the screen, in both orientations. If you cannot, move the holder higher or to the crossbar. Controls you cannot reach quickly are not doing their job.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clamping to something that moves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The deck, motor hood, incline mechanism, lift handle and folding hinge all move during use or folding. A holder fixed to any of them can work loose, trap a cable or drop into the belt. Clamp only to fixed parts of the frame, and fold the machine once with the holder fitted and no tablet in it to check the deck clears it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by screen size alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Two tablets with the same screen can differ in overall size, and a rugged case adds more. Measure the device in its case against the holder's published range, and measure the bar too. A listing that gives neither a physical device range nor a bar diameter is not giving you enough to buy on, however many treadmill brands it names.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for the screen at speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Leaning forward to tap a tablet while running pulls your stride towards the motor hood and off the centre of the belt. Choose what to watch before you start, and drop to a walk before touching the screen. Keep the safety key clipped to your clothing so a stumble stops the belt, and route any charging cable away from it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
